# Images

An image is inline (`![alt](src)`), so it composes with every layout command
in [Commands](commands.sx) — no image-specific syntax needed for sizing or
arrangement.

## Sizing

/skinny(50%)

![a red panda](img.jpg)

## Side by side

// grid(2)

![a red panda](img.jpg)

// --

Here is some caption text, but its not part of the image element.

// end

## Full width

/wide(130%)

![a red panda](img.jpg)

## Captions

A `caption` group wraps the image *before* it in a real `<figure>`/
`<figcaption>` pair — coupled to the image as one element, not a paragraph
that merely sits nearby (`docs/reference/design/018-image-captions-v2.md`;
the full command grammar is in [Commands](commands.sx)). Bare `caption()`
puts it below, matching where a caption is usually expected:

![a red panda](img.jpg)

// caption()
A red panda spotted at the sanctuary, 2024.
// end

`caption(top)` puts it above instead:

![a red panda](img.jpg)

// caption(top)
A red panda spotted at the sanctuary, 2024.
// end

It stacks with sizing commands on the same opener — the caption travels with
the image:

![a red panda](img.jpg)

// skinny(50%) caption()
The same figure, narrowed.
// end

`left`/`right` put the caption in its own column beside the image instead of
below it, sized by a split percent (30% by default). A caption shorter than
the image sits at the bottom of its column rather than floating to the top:

![a red panda](img.jpg)

// caption(left, 35%)
A red panda, 2024.
// end

![a red panda](img.jpg)

// caption(right)
A red panda spotted at the sanctuary during the autumn count, 2024. This one
runs long enough to fill most of the column's height on its own.
// end
